  becoming much more difficult for them  now they might be willing to stay there  if they thought the war was going to be  resolved anytime soon and they could go  home but it's clear the war is getting  worse and Isis is doing very well in  Syria over the past year and is making  rather dramatic gains within Syria in  fact they've even they're approaching  Damascus which i think is one reason the  Russians are going in to try to create a  little enclave for their friend Assad  and when he has to quickly run from  Damascus which will happen in the next  few months I think so from the point of  view of the refugees where they are now  is really not a very good situation at  all because they're not getting the the  carer the attention the food the  medicine medical supplies that they need  in the refugee camps in Turkey Jordan  and Lebanon because the international  aid has been insufficient and they  looked at well and then they well the  donors just haven't come through with as  much as they wildly underestimated how  much they would need no one anticipated  there would be four million refugees in  the nearby countries so the aid has been  insufficient and the country's thus far  I haven't contributed the additional  amount then the refugees look at Syria  and they look at the war and they look  at what's happening in the war and they  realize they're never going to go back  they're never going to be able to go  back the odds that we will have a  peaceful settlement in Syria so that all  of those four million can go home is  unrealistic now and many of them have  lost their homes the war has been  immensely damaging to the housing stock  in the cities and the towns in series so  they look at this situation and a  rational refugee then says well I'm  going to go to Europe where I can get  asylum and so that's been a big I think  it's a sort of a push from given the  developments in the Syrian war and the  situation of refugees and those nearby  countries so does the EU have a policy  for asylum seekers well it does it has a  policy the problem is the policy hasn't  been implemented and and is really not  capable of dealing with the numbers that  we're seeing now I mean the OECD is  estimating that there will be a million  refuge  is coming to europe this year and about  five hundred thousand of them will be  legitimate asylum seekers and and will  get asylum within the EU the EU is  committed to providing asylum meaning  international protection and they've  made it clear that that applies to  everyone who's Syrian but it applies  also to those fleeing Iraq those fleeing  eritrea those three countries are the U  has said are eligible anyone from those  countries is eligible for asylum now the  way it works is the state which receives  the asylum seekers the first state the  frontline state is supposed to register  the people fingerprint them take their  application process the application and  while they're there give them a  temporary residence and if their  application is accepted give them  residence permits for several years the  problem is Greece hasn't done that at  all and I know this is somewhat  facetious to say but basically the  policy of the tsipras government in  dealing with the refugees Syrian  refugees coming from Turkey has been to  put up a road sign pointing to Macedonia  and and just telling them to go north to  Macedonia and then they go through  Macedonian north to Serbia and then they  get too hungry and of course we've seen  what's happened in hungry which has felt  overwhelmed and put up barbed wire  fences and so forth so they've taken a  left at hungry and gone to Croatia and  Croatia's received something like 70,000  refugees and you know in the past week  and it's overwhelmed as well all of the  countries now are putting up border  controls and  if they're if they're good Europeans of  putting up border controls reasserting  border controls which Schengen the free  movement provisions allow in emergencies  there are some countries such as hungry  an outlier which is building fences and  is now building a fence having built the  fence between itself and serbia's  building one between itself and Romania  and one between itself and in Croatia so  the Balkans are having a great deal of  difficulty dealing with this and it's  been in that context that the Interior  Minister is justice and Home Affairs  ministers met yesterday and the leaders  of the EU are meeting today and what do  you think the outcome will be well  yesterday we had a really very  interesting and unusual meeting prior to  the prior to that meeting the EU had  agreed starting last spring and through  the summer to develop a plan to  distribute the refugees on a quota basis  to all of the Member States and they  plan to distribute 40,000 from Italy and  Greece to the Member States and this was  prompted by the flow that came across  the Mediterranean last spring when the  boat loads were coming across and people  were many boats were sinking they were  terrible boats they a lot of them were  over overloaded they were people were  drowning so several thousand people died  in the course of that and at that point  Greece and Italy which were the prime  recipients of the refugees had this plan  developed or the EU developed it to  relieve them by taking the refugees and  distributing them throughout the EU it  was basically similar to a plan the  Germans developed in 1949  used again in 1989 when they got a flow  of people coming from the east used it  again in the Balkans wars of the early  90s in which Germany developed a formula  to distribute silam seekers throughout  the country taking into account of for  example distributing among the states  according to the wealth of the other the  tax revenues of the of the state and the  size of the states within Germany and  they've basically adapted that to the EU  so that we they now have a quota for  those 40,000 well for the last several  weeks there's 